  "analysis": "We need to implement an efficient solution for the problem Subset Sums (Codeforces 348C). The constraints require handling up to 1e5 elements, sets, and queries with a total of set memberships up to 1e5. A sqrt-decomposition approach is appropriate: classify sets into big (heavy) and small (light) by a threshold B ~ sqrt(total memberships). For big sets, maintain a lazy add value and a maintained sum for quick queries. For small sets, update the underlying element values directly and update the sums for all big sets using precomputed intersections. We precompute: (1) for every set k and every big set B the intersection size interCounts[k][B], (2) the intersection matrix between big sets interBigMatrix[B1][B2], and (3) per-element lists of which big sets contain that element (bigInElem). We also maintain a current per-element array a[i] that stores initial values plus updates from small sets only, and a per-big-set sum sumBigVal[B] that represents the current sum of values in big set B (including contributions from small updates and from big-set updates). Updates on small sets adjust a[i] and update sumBigVal for all big sets using interCounts; updates on big sets adjust addBig and propagate to sumBigVal using interBigMatrix. Queries return quickly either by summing small set elements plus big contributions, or by reading sumBigVal for big sets.\n\nPlan:\n- Read input, store all sets.\n- Choose threshold B = floor(sqrt(total_memberships)) + 1.\n- Mark sets as big if size > B. Build data structures for big sets: bigSets, bigIndex, bigInElem (for each element, which big sets contain it).\n- Build interCounts[k][b] = |Sk ∩ BigSet[b]| for all k, b, by iterating elements and their big-set memberships.\n- Build interBigMatrix[B1][B2] by per-element accumulation from bigInElem.\n- Initialize a[i] with initial values, sumBigVal[B] as the sum of a[i] over i ∈ BigSet[B], and addBig[B] = 0.\n- Process queries:\n  - For + k x:\n    - If k is big: addBig[bi] += x; for all big B: sumBigVal[B] += x * interBigMatrix[B][bi]\n    - If k is small: for i ∈ Sk: a[i] += x; for all big B: sumBigVal[B] += x * interCounts[k][B]\n  - For ? k:\n    - If k is big: output sumBigVal[bigIndex[k]]\n    - If k is small: compute res = sum of a[i] for i ∈ Sk; then add contributions from big updates: sum over all big B of addBig[B] * interCounts[k][B]. Output res.\n- Use 64-bit integers for sums.\n\nThis approach ensures near O(|Sk| + #Big) work per small update, O(numBig) per big update, and O(|Sk| + #Big) per small query, which fits within limits given total memberships ≤ 1e5 and #Big ≤ ~316.",
