  "analysis": "The task is to implement a simulator for pouring water into n vessels with overflow to the next vessel. A straightforward per-query simulation can be too slow if we repeatedly traverse full vessels. The standard efficient approach uses a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) (also called a "next pointer" structure) to skip vessels that are already full. Each vessel i has capacity a[i] and current water w[i]. We keep a DSU parent[] where find(i) returns the next vessel at or after i that is not yet full; when a vessel becomes full, we union it with i+1. Pouring x liters into p repeats: find the next non-full vessel i >= p, fill as much as possible, and if i becomes full, union and continue with the next non-full vessel. Each vessel becomes full at most once, so total complexity is near-linear in n + m. For queries of type 2, we just output w[k]. The solution will be implemented in C++17 for speed and clarity.",
  "plan": "1) Implement DSU-based skipping: parent array with path compression, find(x) returns the first non-full vessel >= x. 2) On pour query (type 1): while there is water to pour and we haven't passed n, find the next non-full vessel i = find(p). Compute can = a[i] - w[i], pour take = min(can, x). Update w[i] and x. If vessel i becomes full, union it with i+1 by parent[i] = find(i+1). Continue with the next non-full vessel. 3) On query (type 2): print w[k]. 4) Edge cases: 1-indexed vessels, sentinel at n+1, long long for capacities and water. 5) Provide a self-contained C++17 solution and a few commands to compile and test with sample inputs.",
